# Style and documentation

## File / listing organisation

- First line is a comment with unit name, optional page number, date.
- One definition per line; subsequent lines indent **3 spaces** (multiples
  of 3 for nested control). No blank lines between consecutive definitions
  -- only between groups.
- Keep `decimal` at start *and* end. If a section uses `hex`, wrap it:
  `base @ >r  hex  …  r> base !`.
- Declare data one per line with a one-line comment. Initialise variables
  in one `init` word called from the load file.

For naming, prefixes, and suffixes see `forth-naming.mdc`.

## Definition layout

```forth
: example ( u -- flag )
   0= if  false exit  then
   true ;
```

`:` at column 0; body indented 3 spaces. Target one line, ~7 references;
never more than ~3 levels of nested control per word (deeper = missing
factoring).

## Forth-natural syntax

Keep words composable on the stack:

- Numbers precede names (`20 spaces`, not `spaces 20`).
- Address before count (`( c-addr u )` for `type`, `erase`, `blank`).
- Source before destination (`( src dst u ) move`).
- Zero-relative indexing inside; convert at the user edge only.
- Avoid parsing words that expect a specific follower in the input stream.

## Stack comments

Every definition that touches the stack carries a stack-effect comment.
Five comment kinds, used together as needed:

| kind            | example                              |
|-----------------|--------------------------------------|
| stack-effect    | `( c-addr u -- flag )`               |
| data-structure  | `\ { 1count \| 63text }`             |
| input-stream    | `( "name" -- xt )`                   |
| purpose         | `\ restore base after dump`          |
| narrative       | inside body, vertical format only    |

Use `\` for descriptive text and whole-line comment-out; use `( … )` for inline stack pictures and sub-line snippets. Do not nest `( … )` — the first `)` ends the comment. Avoid `)` in word names.

When effects differ per path, list each on its own line with full `--`:
`( -- c-addr u true | -- ior false )`. When `>r` `r>` `r@` are used, add
`(R … )` on the header or the line above the first `>r`.

## Stack abbreviations (App E)

`n` signed cell · `u` unsigned · `d`/`ud` double · `c` char · `b` byte ·
`a`/`addr` address · `'x` address of `x` · `?`/`t=`/`f=` flag · `#` count ·
`i` index (elements) · `o` offset (bytes) · `m` mask · `x` don't-care.
Input stream: `c` blank-delimited char · `name` word · `text<delim>` string
with explicit delimiter.

## Magic numbers and locals

Use constants, not unexplained literals. Keep about 3 items live on the
data stack inside one definition; for a worst-case primitive with 4+
arguments, use Gforth locals (see `forth-dialect-gforth.mdc`):

```forth
42 constant asterisk-code
: star ( -- )  asterisk-code emit ;

: [box] { x1 y1 x2 y2 -- }
  x1 y1 x2 y1 line   x2 y1 x2 y2 line
  x2 y2 x1 y2 line   x1 y2 x1 y1 line ;
```

## Structured programming

One entry, one normal exit. Use `catch` / `throw` (or the project's IOR
convention) for errors -- not ad-hoc jumps out of nested structures.
